From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] sparc32: keep the Forth heap inside the firmware's own
 virtual range

OpenBIOS mapped its 128K Forth heap (alloc-mem) right below 0xffd00000,
outside the 2 MB from 0xffd00000 that ofmem_init() reserves for the
firmware. A Sun PROM keeps all its mappings between 0xffd00000 and
0xfff00000. The kernels of Solaris 2.3 and 2.4 put their kernelmap at
0xfc000000-0xffd00000 and take every hole in /virtual-memory "available"
inside it out of the map; a hole that reaches past 0xffd00000 can't be,
and they panic: "panic - kernel: can't remove OBP hole". Solaris 2.5 and
later put the kernelmap lower and never see it.

Map the heap above the I/O mappings, inside the reserved range, when it
fits there (it does: about 200K are free above them); otherwise where it
was before.
---
 arch/sparc32/openbios.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/sparc32/openbios.c b/arch/sparc32/openbios.c
index 17d47a9..abdb799 100644
--- a/arch/sparc32/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/sparc32/openbios.c
@@ -802,7 +802,15 @@ static void init_memory(void)
     if (!phys)
         printk("panic: not enough physical memory on host system.\n");
     
-    virt = ofmem_claim_virt(OF_CODE_START - MEMORY_SIZE, MEMORY_SIZE, 0);
+    /* Put it above the I/O mappings, in the 2 MB from OF_CODE_START that
+       ofmem_init() keeps for OpenBIOS: a Sun PROM keeps all its mappings
+       between 0xffd00000 and 0xfff00000. The Solaris 2.3 and 2.4 kernels have
+       their kernelmap right below 0xffd00000 and panic ("can't remove OBP
+       hole") on a PROM mapping there. */
+    virt = ofmem_arch_get_iomem_top();
+    if (virt + MEMORY_SIZE > OF_CODE_START + 0x200000) {
+        virt = ofmem_claim_virt(OF_CODE_START - MEMORY_SIZE, MEMORY_SIZE, 0);
+    }
     if (!virt)
         printk("panic: not enough virtual memory on host system.\n");
 
